  "prompt_text": "The figure is a schematic overview of many human-made sources of explosive or shock-generating events across land, air, space, and the ocean. It groups these sources by environment, showing that strong transient signals can come from very different activities but all couple energy into the ground, atmosphere, or water.\n\nOn land, the image highlights rockets, surface and buried chemical experiments, industrial explosions, military explosions, and mine collapses. Cross-sectional cutaways show underground shafts and tunnels, emphasizing that some events occur below the surface and can produce signals through the surrounding rock. At the surface, factories, vehicles, and visible fireballs suggest accidental or intentional blasts.\n\nAbove the ground and sea, the figure also includes re-entry vehicles and aircraft accidents, indicating airborne or impact-related sources. Offshore and underwater, it shows a ship conducting underwater shock tests, submarine accidents, and even vulnerable seafloor pipelines labeled as critical infrastructure. The sloping coastline links the terrestrial and marine settings into one continuous scene.\n\nOverall, the main message is that hazardous or detectable explosive events arise in multiple domains, and their effects propagate through different media depending on where the event occurs.",
